  "issues": [
    "COVERAGE GUARANTEE LOGICAL GAP [critical, newly identified]: The proposal's primary theoretical claim — 'SCG enforces proportional coverage by construction: the grammar mandates that each mandatory role appears in every generated instance' — is overstated given confirmed RAMS TSV structure. The RAMS ontology TSV (independently verified at nlp.jhu.edu/rams/) specifies only MAXIMUM cardinality (the 'count permitted' column), NOT minimum cardinality. XGrammar therefore enforces upper-bound cardinality and entity-type constraints, but CANNOT force optional roles to appear. Since rare roles in RAMS may be optional in many event types (a given event instance may legitimately not have an 'Instrument' or 'Beneficiary'), the grammar provides no coverage guarantee for those roles. The coverage guarantee claim holds only for roles that the paper explicitly sets min-cardinality=1 in the grammar — which would require an assumption not derivable from the RAMS TSV alone and would produce unrealistic training data (every instance must have every role filled). The proposal must either (a) acknowledge this and narrow the guarantee to 'roles assigned min-cardinality=1 in the grammar encoding' with an explicit design choice described, or (b) reframe the theoretical argument from 'structural coverage guarantee' to 'structurally-prompted generation is more likely to instantiate optional rare roles due to explicit slot salience' — an empirical hypothesis rather than a logical guarantee. This is the single issue that a RAMS-familiar reviewer will raise immediately.",
    "BoostCD unacknowledged [minor]: 'Combining Constrained and Unconstrained Decoding via Boosting: BoostCD and Its Application to Information Extraction' (EMNLP 2025 Main, arXiv:2506.14901) empirically demonstrates that constrained and unconstrained decoding errors are complementary for closed IE, with a learned boosted model combining both. While BoostCD targets inference-time decoding (not training-data generation), its central empirical finding — that hard constraints and unconstrained generation have complementary error profiles — is directly relevant to CompSchema-IE's motivating argument and should be cited in related work with a one-sentence differentiation.",
    "Training volume N unspecified [minor]: The proposal never states the target training volume N. For RAMS with 139 event types, the number of instances per type and total N are unknown, making the GPU cost estimates (37.5h for 1.5N pool, 25h for N SCG-constrained) unverifiable. Specify N upfront (e.g., 'N=5,000 instances distributed proportionally across event types') so compute estimates and reproducibility can be assessed."
  ],
  "feedback": "The Round 6 proposal is the strongest version yet — all five Round 5 issues are properly resolved (volume normalization via 1.5× pool, FineCSDA cited and differentiated, null result pre-specified, pilot study at 600 instances, BiTer protocol mismatch handled). DEGAP F1 numbers (RAMS 54.2, WikiEvents 67.1 Arg-C) are independently verified correct. XGrammar overhead claim (<40μs/token as default vLLM backend) is confirmed. One fixable critical issue blocks submission readiness: the coverage guarantee must be corrected.\n\n(1) REVISE THE COVERAGE GUARANTEE ARGUMENT [critical]: The RAMS TSV only provides maximum cardinality (upper bounds on role occurrences), not minimum cardinality. The grammar therefore enforces: (a) no role appears more than its max count, and (b) when filled, entity types are compatible. It CANNOT guarantee that rare optional roles appear at all. Replace 'SCG enforces proportional coverage by construction' with one of two framings: OPTION A — 'In our grammar encoding, we set min-cardinality=1 for all roles applicable to a given event type, guaranteeing their appearance in every generated instance; this is a design choice that differs from natural annotation practice and we evaluate whether this artificial completeness improves rare-role coverage downstream.' OPTION B — 'SCG provides structural salience: by enumerating every applicable role slot explicitly in the constrained generation template, the LLM is prompted to consider filling each slot rather than freely omitting rare ones; we test empirically whether this structured attention mechanism improves rare-role instantiation.' Option B is more honest and requires no changes to the actual method.\n\n(2) ADD BoostCD [one sentence in related work]: 'Concurrently, BoostCD (EMNLP 2025) demonstrates that constrained and unconstrained decoding produce complementary errors at inference time for closed IE; CompSchema-IE investigates an analogous question at training-data generation time, where coverage differences compound across a finite corpus.'\n\n(3) SPECIFY N [one sentence in Section 2 or Step 2]: State the target training volume N explicitly (e.g., 'N=5,000 instances drawn proportionally across RAMS event types') so compute estimates are verifiable and the paper is reproducible.\n\nWith these three changes — particularly the coverage guarantee reframing — the proposal is ready for EMNLP 2026 Findings submission. The empirical design is sound, the null result handling is appropriate, and the resource estimate is within budget.",
  "reason": "All Round 5 issues resolved; one new critical logical gap in the coverage guarantee claim (RAMS TSV gives only max cardinality, not min, so XGrammar cannot force optional rare roles to appear), plus two minor gaps (BoostCD citation, volume N unspecified) — fixable in one revision pass."
}
